# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/007_enum_value_arithmetic.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_enum_value_arithmetic
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Enumeration constants in arithmetic and enumerated-type underlying behaviour, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every enumerator is small and every arithmetic result is far inside the range of int, so no
signed type overflows: the largest magnitude occurring anywhere in the program, printed or
intermediate, is 300. No shift operator appears at all, so no shift count can be out of
range.
Both array subscripts are known enumerators inside the bounds of a three element array, and
the two forms differ deliberately: the folded subscript is the enumeration constant SEQ_C,
which is 2, while the runtime subscript is r_seq_b, staged from a volatile object holding
SEQ_B, which is 1. Neither can reach index 3, so no out-of-bounds access occurs and
AddressSanitizer stays clean.
The switch in classify covers all three enumerators of enum Color and is still followed by a
return statement, so no path falls off the end of a non-void function whatever value reaches
it.
NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER, and that is enforced structurally rather
than argued. An access to a volatile object is an observable side effect, and C11 6.5.2.2p10
leaves the order of evaluation of a call's function designator, of its arguments, and of
subexpressions within those arguments unspecified. No call in this program therefore reads
more than one volatile object: every volatile enumerated object is read exactly once, in a
full expression of its own, into a plain int -- r_red, r_green, r_blue, r_seq_b, r_gap_next
and r_gap_last -- and each classify call is likewise made in a statement of its own into
r_class_red, r_class_green and r_class_blue. Every printf below then receives plain objects
only, so no argument list contains a side effect at all and the suite's authoring rule of at
most one side-effecting argument per call is satisfied by construction rather than by an
argument about distinct objects. Staging costs the test nothing, which is why it is the right
answer rather than a compromise: a value that arrived through a volatile load stays opaque to
the optimizer, so every printed runtime value is still computed at run time rather than folded.
The three type-probe functions are each the sole argument of their own printf, and the one
volatile object among them -- enum_object_type's local c -- is written once by its own
initializer and never read at run time, because a _Generic controlling expression is not
evaluated.
Otherwise: no aliasing violation, no uninitialized read -- every volatile object and the
table are initialized in their own declarations -- and no dependence on padding bytes or on
the addresses of unrelated objects.
Every volatile enumerated value is converted to int by an explicit cast at the point it is
staged -- or, for the run_switch values, by classify's prototyped enum Color parameter and its
int return -- and the staged objects are themselves declared int, so every value reaching a %d
conversion specification has type int with no promotion left to happen at the call site. The
size relation is printed through a cast as well, and the enumeration constants already have
type int and need no cast. The complete default warning gate passes on this program with no
diagnostic and no deviation, measured with gcc 13.4.0, which is why this record declares no
ub_audit_flags at all.
The three _Generic controlling expressions are not evaluated (C11 6.5.1.1p3), so they report
types without performing a conversion or a volatile access at run time.
Every arithmetic property is computed twice, once from enumeration constants the folder can
evaluate at compile time and once from volatile-qualified storage that forces a run-time
read, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the one the backend computes. The
size relation is the single property with no runtime twin, because sizeof is evaluated at
compile time by definition.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Two implementation-defined properties are navigated by authoring technique rather than
tested naively, and neither is a reason to restrict the target list or to switch an oracle
off: all four targets stay fully compared under all three oracles.
First, the enumerated type's compatible type. C11 6.7.2.2p4 leaves the type compatible with
an enumerated type implementation-defined, so listing both enum Color and int among the
associations of one _Generic is a constraint violation wherever those two turn out to be
compatible, and a conforming implementation must reject that form rather than pick a winner. The
portable form used here therefore lists only the enumerated type itself plus default. The
two probes that do report int are portable for reasons that do not depend on the compatible
type at all: an identifier declared as an enumeration constant has type int by C11 6.4.4.3p2,
which is what makes the RED probe report int, and int can represent every value of enum
Color, so the integer promotions give a + 0 the type int by C11 6.3.1.1p2 whatever the
compatible type happens to be.
Second, the enumerated type's width, which follows from that same implementation-defined
choice. It is never printed as a number. It is asserted only as the relation
sizeof(enum Color) == sizeof(int), printed as a 0/1 comparison, and that relation was
measured to hold on all four supported targets, both sizes being 4, which is what makes the
line comparable across backends. Printing the width itself would risk reporting a purely
implementation-defined difference as a finding against a backend that did nothing wrong.
The negative enumerator BLUE = -3 removes a further class of variation: an implementation's
choice of compatible type must be able to represent every enumerator (C11 6.7.2.2p4), so a
negative enumerator forces a signed type and the sign stops being a per-target choice.
No plain char object is declared and no plain-char value is ever read as a NUMBER, because
plain char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64. Character data is present -- printf's format parameter and the string literals the
three type probes return are arrays of char reached through const char * -- and %s does read
and print those bytes; what never happens is a plain-char value being converted to an integer
type, used as an arithmetic operand, compared, or formatted numerically, so the signedness
choice cannot change one byte of this program's output. long and unsigned long are never used
at all, because their width was measured as 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the other three
targets. No pointer VALUE is printed: %s prints the bytes a pointer designates, never the
pointer itself.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_values=1 5 -3
fold_seq=0 1 2
fold_gap=10 11 20 21
fold_difference=4
fold_product=-6
fold_total=3
fold_index=33
fold_relation=1
fold_size_relation=1
run_values=1 5 -3
run_seq=1
run_gap=11 21
run_difference=4
run_product=-6
run_total=3
run_index=22
run_relation=1
run_switch=100 200 300
enumerator_type=int
enum_object_type=enum Color
enum_sum_type=int
END
